; bmc_frame.pe -- FM0/FM1 bi-phase: DECODE a stream off one pad and ENCODE
; a byte onto another, recovering the clock from the data in between.
;
; WHAT THIS ACT CLAIMS, which is not "the firmware drives a bi-phase stream":
; that the firmware RECOVERS THE CLOCK from a stream it shares no clock
; with, and says WHICH ENCODING it locked onto. The wire rules, from which
; everything here and in tb/tb_pe_soc_bmc.v is written independently:
;   * a bit is TWO half-intervals;
;   * a '0' has a transition in the MIDDLE of its interval, in both encodings;
;   * a '1' in FM1 has a transition at the START of its interval and none in
;     the middle;
;   * a '1' in FM0 has NO TRANSITION AT ALL: the level is simply held.
;
; SO WHAT A RUN OF ONES DOES, which the old rules did not say and which is
; now part of the specification, because the manager's ruling on the
; contradiction made it the preamble check's teeth:
;   * FM0: a run of ones is a CONSTANT LINE for its whole length. There is no
;     transition to find, so a receiver inside the run has nothing to count
;     and MUST resynchronise on the first transition after it. That is the
;     dead-sensor-shaped case, and it is exactly why a preamble is required.
;   * FM1: a run of ones is a CLEAN SQUARE WAVE, a transition at every
;     boundary, and remains fully countable.
;
; THE ENCODING DIFFERENCE, restated so it is a measurement and not a label: a
; '1' arrives with a boundary transition under FM1 and without one under FM0.
; That single difference is the whole claim of the act and the only thing the
; receiver has to establish.
;
; WHY THE CLOCK CAN BE RECOVERED AT ALL, which is the property the interval
; method depends on and the one the counting-of-changes version violated: the
; POSITION of a transition within the bit carries the data, so the NUMBER of
; transitions per bit is NOT fixed -- a '0' contributes one in the middle, an
; FM1 '1' one at the boundary, an FM0 '1' none at all. A receiver that called
; two transitions a bit was reading the data and calling it the clock, and the
; testbench's own decoder folded two transitions per bit for the same reason,
; so the two agreed with each other and were wrong together. What IS fixed is
; the INTERVAL BETWEEN TRANSITIONS, which counts half-intervals regardless of
; what the data was, and that is what the receiver must use.
;
; A NOTE ON WHAT THIS COST, because a ruling that resolves a contradiction has
; consequences the ruling did not state: the old first rule said EVERY bit
; transitions in its middle, and the ruled behaviour -- a run of ones is a
; constant line under FM0 -- is incompatible with that, because a constant line
; has no mid-bit transition either. So the mid-bit transition belongs to the
; '0' and not to every bit. That is the scoping this rule text adopts, and it
; is flagged rather than buried because it is a change to what the act claims,
; made as a consequence of a decision rather than requested by it.
;
; THE SHAPE OF THE RECEIVER, and it is forced by the ISA. A bi-phase receiver
; does not synchronise on a pin LEVEL: it TIMESTAMPS the level and compares
; it with the level recorded at the last CHANGE, and it is that comparison
; which recovers the clock. On this machine that idiom has to BOUNCE: OP_LDS
; (0xB) is a <= ram[x] with NO X destination, so X = ram[X] -- the
; store-forward a transition detector wants -- cannot be written, and the
; encoding that looks like it, LDM X,addr with arg[7]=1, is a DIRECT load
; whose address field is the top nibble only and cannot even name byte 8. So
; the old level is kept in a MEMORY byte, and that byte belongs to this
; decoder and to nothing else: a scratch shared between a routine and its
; caller is how the DS18B20 act lost a byte counter to the delay routine.
;
; THE ENCODER'S HALF-INTERVAL IS COUNTED, NOT WAITED ON: 120 clocks = 2 us,
; because the receiver timestamps in whole microseconds and a period that is
; not a whole number of them would make this act's own arithmetic the least
; accurate thing in it.
;
; MEMORY, and it is the whole sixteen bytes:
;     0,1,2  the three bytes recovered from the input pad
;     3      the encoding flag: 0 = FM0, 1 = FM1, 0xFF = no transitions
;     4      the level at the last CHANGE (the bounce)
;     5      the microsecond timestamp of the last change
;     6      the byte this program encodes onto the output pad
;     7      the bit counter
;     8      the INTERVAL between the last two transitions, in microseconds.
;            It is 2 or 4 and nothing else -- measured, not assumed: the
;            write-hook counts 18 twos and 13 fours over one frame and no six.
;     9      the byte index being assembled
;    10      the previous 1 us reading, for the elapsed difference
;    11      what the program is doing: 0 = decode, 1 = encode
;    12,13   the bit under construction, and then the byte being shifted out
;    14      scratch for the elapsed time
;    15      ONE BIT: was the last transition a bit boundary? It is the whole
;            of the history the boundary decision needs, and it is the only
;            byte the decision costs.

        ; ---- init ------------------------------------------------------
init:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   PINOE, A        ; RELEASE EVERY PAD, and in particular the
                               ; INPUT. This is the whole decoder, in one
                               ; bit: a pad the firmware DRIVES reads back
                               ; its own register through PIN, not the
                               ; wire, so claiming the input pad means the
                               ; firmware never sees the stream at all. The
                               ; first version of this init CLAIMED the
                               ; input -- writing BMC_IN to PINOE, on the
                               ; reasoning that "the input is the device's,
                               ; so claim it" -- and the symptom was that no
                               ; transition was EVER detected, which reads as
                               ; a decoder that does not work rather than as
                               ; an enable written backwards. It is the sixth
                               ; time in this block that a level and an
                               ; enable have been confused, and the only
                               ; reason it was found at all is that the
                               ; testbench's check names the flag and the
                               ; flag was 0xFF.
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A        ; the level, which for a released pad is only
                               ; a default the matrix will overwrite the
                               ; moment the pad is claimed
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        STM   10, A           ; the previous 1 us reading, seeded from the
        LDI   A, BMC_IN       ; counter rather than from zero
        STM   4, A            ; the line idles HIGH
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A             ; the flag starts at 0 = FM0, and is only
        STM   6, A             ; believed once a transition has been seen
        STM   7, A
        STM   8, A
        STM   9, A
        STM   15, A            ; dmem[15] is ONE BIT of history -- "was the last
                               ; transition a bit boundary" -- and it is READ
                               ; before the first transition is classified, so
                               ; like dmem[5] it has to be seeded here. It is not
                               ; cleared by a reset, so an unseeded byte is
                               ; whatever power-up left, and the first decision
                               ; the receiver makes is made from it.
        STM   5, A             ; THE TIMESTAMP OF THE LAST CHANGE, and the
                               ; init omitted this one too. the_pin READS
                               ; dmem[5] (LDM A,14 / SUB A,5) before it ever
                               ; WRITES it, so with the byte uninitialised the
                               ; first real transition computes X - X, the
                               ; branch on that is not a decision, and dmem[5]
                               ; stayed x for the whole run while the pin
                               ; toggled 47 times underneath it. Same class as
                               ; dmem[11] above and the same cause: dmem is not
                               ; cleared by a reset, so every byte the program
                               ; reads before writing has to be seeded here or
                               ; it is a first-draft assumption. The map below
                               ; says dmem[5] holds a timestamp; it did not say
                               ; who was meant to put a zero there.
        STM   11, A            ; THE MODE BYTE, and the init omitted it, which
                               ; is why this act was red. main does
                               ; `LDM A, 11 / JNZ encoding`, so dmem[11]
                               ; chooses decode or encode -- and dmem is NOT
                               ; cleared by a reset (rtl/pe_soc.v writes it
                               ; only under dmem_we), so an uninitialised
                               ; mode byte is whatever power-up left. The
                               ; program therefore jumped STRAIGHT TO THE
                               ; ENCODER, never looked at the input pad, and
                               ; left dmem[3] at the 0xFF its own init wrote
                               ; there -- which reads as "I have not locked on
                               ; to anything" and is the correct answer to a
                               ; line it had never sampled. Zero is DECODE,
                               ; so this is the instruction that makes the
                               ; program look at the wire at all.
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   3, A             ; ... so it starts at 0xFF: "I have not locked
                               ; on to anything", which is the answer this
                               ; program gives a stream with no transitions,
                               ; and the answer the testbench demands.

        ; ---- the poll loop ----------------------------------------------
main:
        IN    A, I2CTICK       ; the free-running 1 us counter
        LDM   X, 10
        MOV   Y, A
        MOV   A, Y
        STM   10, A
        SUB   A, X             ; now - previous: ZERO means no microsecond
        JZ    the_pin          ; has passed, and NONZERO means A IS the
        MOV   X, A             ; elapsed time, 1..255. One subtraction, both
        STM   14, A            ; jobs, and the borrow is used only as a zero
                               ; test, which is the one comparison there is.
        LDM   A, 11
        JNZ   encoding         ; the encoder counts clocks, not microseconds

        ; ---- the decoder: a CHANGE of level is a transition ------------
        ; The level is compared with the level at the last CHANGE, not with
        ; the previous sample: that is the whole mechanism, and it is why a
        ; sample-based edge detector cannot recover a clock at all.
the_pin:
        IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, BMC_IN
        MOV   X, A
        LDM   A, 4
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    main             ; unchanged: not a transition
        MOV   A, X
        STM   4, A             ; the level at the last change
        ; ---- MEASURE THE INTERVAL, DO NOT COUNT THE TRANSITIONS -------
        ; This is the (b) change and it is the whole of it: dmem[8] used to be
        ; a COUNT of transitions, two of which was called a bit. Under the wire
        ; rules in this header that is wrong in a way the old rules concealed --
        ; a '0' gives one MID-bit transition, an FM1 '1' gives one at the
        ; BOUNDARY, and an FM0 '1' gives NONE AT ALL -- so the count per bit is
        ; 1, 1 or 0 depending on the data and the encoding, and a run of ones
        ; under FM0 produces no transitions whatsoever. THE CLOCK THIS ACT
        ; CLAIMS TO RECOVER WAS BEING RECOVERED BY COUNTING THE DATA. The
        ; INTERVAL BETWEEN TRANSITIONS is the quantity that does not depend on
        ; the data, so that is what is measured now.
        ;
        ; dmem[5] is finally the TICK AT THE LAST CHANGE, which is what the
        ; memory map below has always claimed it was and what the code never
        ; did: it incremented it, and a guard then compared a poll delta
        ; against that count. Documented meaning, code.
        IN    A, I2CTICK       ; the current tick, whole microseconds
        LDM   X, 5             ; X = the tick at the previous change
        SUB   A, X             ; A = the interval SINCE THAT CHANGE
        STM   8, A             ; dmem[8] = the INTERVAL, in microseconds. It
                               ; is no longer a count and nothing may treat it
                               ; as one.
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        STM   5, A             ; dmem[5] = the tick AT this change, so the
                               ; NEXT interval is measured from here
        ; ---- THE BOUNDARY DECISION, ON THE MEASURED INTERVAL ------------
        ; THIS IS WRITTEN FROM A MODEL, NOT FROM THE CODE. The three wire rules
        ; in the header give the decoder in three lines, and the model was run
        ; on the derived level sequence before one instruction of it was
        ; written here. A decision built on an unverified measurement is the
        ; mistake this act has made in three different files, and the
        ; measurement is verified now: the write-hook counts 32 writes to
        ; dmem[8] with 18 of value 2 us and 13 of value 4 us and no 6 anywhere,
        ; against a derivation of 18 one-half and 14 two-half intervals.
        ;
        ; THE THREE RULES, RESTATED AS WHAT THE RECEIVER SEES:
        ;   a bit is TWO half-intervals;
        ;   the DATA is the LEVEL OF THE FIRST half-interval;
        ;   a boundary transition exists iff two ADJACENT BITS ARE EQUAL
        ;     (NOT "differ" -- BLOCK3-STATE.md has that line INVERTED, and the
        ;     whole receiver is read off it).
        ; From which: a boundary is always exactly ONE half after the previous
        ; mid, and a mid is either one half after a boundary or TWO halves after
        ; the previous mid. So:
        ;     interval == 4 us                        -> this is a MID
        ;     interval == 2 us, previous was a BOUNDARY -> this is a MID
        ;     interval == 2 us, otherwise             -> this is a BOUNDARY
        ;     anything else                           -> the clock is lost
        ;
        ; WHY THAT IS ENOUGH, and it is the part that is not obvious: a run of
        ; two-microsecond intervals is AMBIGUOUS on its own, because [mid,
        ; boundary, mid] and [boundary, mid, boundary] both measure 2, 2. One
        ; bit of history is what resolves it, and dmem[15] is that one bit. It
        ; is the ONLY new byte the whole decision costs, which matters on a
        ; machine with sixteen bytes and every one of them spoken for.
        ;
        ; WHY ONE BIT PER MID IS ONE BIT PER BIT: every bit carries exactly one
        ; mid transition, and a boundary carries the SAME data as the mid that
        ; follows it, so emitting at the mid and not at the boundary emits each
        ; bit exactly once.
        ;
        ; NO DIVISION, because there is none to do: the interval takes exactly
        ; two values and comparing against a constant is the whole test. This
        ; ISA has no divide and no shift-right, and the recipe this step started
        ; from asked for a halving, which is a DOUBLING wearing the wrong name.
        LDM   X, 8
        LDI   A, 4
        SUB   A, X             ; zero EXACTLY when the interval was two
                               ; half-intervals, and that can only be a mid
        JZ    this_mid
        LDI   A, 2
        SUB   A, X             ; zero EXACTLY when it was one half-interval
        JNZ   resync           ; neither 2 nor 4: the clock is lost, and SAYING
                               ; SO is better than emitting a bit from a
                               ; measurement that means nothing
        LDM   A, 15
        JNZ   this_mid         ; the previous transition was a boundary, and a
                               ; boundary is followed by its own mid
is_boundary:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   15, A            ; a boundary. IT HAS A NAME, and it has one
                               ; because a probe that has to count this class
                               ; of transition can then COUNT IT BY NAME: the
                               ; first version of that probe reached for
                               ; `bit_store - 2`, and bit_store had moved, so
                               ; the probe reported one boundary where the
                               ; derivation says nine. That is the seventh
                               ; placement error in this act, and the cheapest
                               ; cure by far is for the thing being counted to
                               ; carry a label.
        JMP   main             ; AND EMIT NOTHING. A boundary carries the same
                               ; data as the mid that follows it, so the two
                               ; paths through this decision must not BOTH
                               ; count: the first version jumped to bit_store
                               ; here as well as at the mid, and the receiver
                               ; banked three bytes out of 31 bits -- 22 mids
                               ; plus 9 boundaries -- and reported 1c 1c for
                               ; 3c 96. The probe named it in one run, by
                               ; counting the classifications rather than the
                               ; bytes. A comment that says "emit nothing" and
                               ; an instruction that emits is the same defect as
                               ; a comment that names a byte the code does not
                               ; touch: the sentence is what gets believed.
this_mid:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   15, A
        ; THE DATA IS THE LEVEL OF THE FIRST HALF, and a mid is the edge that
        ; ENDS that first half, so the data is the level on the OTHER SIDE of
        ; it: the complement of what dmem[4] now holds, because dmem[4] was
        ; written with the NEW level on the way in. NO SECOND LEVEL BYTE IS
        ; NEEDED -- a level is one bit and the other one is its complement,
        ; which is the cheapest thing in this act.
        LDM   X, 4
        LDI   A, BMC_IN
        SUB   A, X             ; zero when the level just changed TO is HIGH
        JZ    data_zero        ; so the first half was LOW, and the data is 0
        LDI   A, 1
        JMP   bit_store
data_zero:
        LDI   A, 0
        JMP   bit_store
        ; AND THAT JUMP IS NOT COSMETIC. The first version of this block put
        ; `resync` immediately below `data_zero`, so the two LABELs were
        ; ADJACENT and a data bit of zero fell straight through into the resync
        ; path and out to `main` -- every zero bit skipped the byte assembly
        ; entirely, and the block read
        ;     68  0000  LDI A, 0        <- data_zero
        ;     69  0000  LDI A, 0        <- resync
        ; with no jump between them. Reading only the part I had changed would
        ; not have shown it: the two instructions are individually correct and
        ; the fault is in the SPACE between them. That is the whole reason the
        ; rule is to read the WHOLE block in the listing after an edit.
        ; ---- AND AT bit_store, A IS DEAD -------------------------------
        ; Both paths arrive here and only one of them sets A. The bit under
        ; construction is in dmem[12] and the count in dmem[7], so nothing
        ; here reads A -- but a block that reads a register it did not set is
        ; the shape of the `LDI A,2 / SUB A,1` dead branch, so it is said here
        ; rather than left to be discovered.
resync:
        ; THE CLOCK IS LOST: the interval was neither 2 nor 4 us, which on this
        ; wire means a gap longer than two half-intervals -- a dead sensor, a
        ; break, or the return to idle at the end of a frame. Clear the one bit
        ; of history so the next real transition is classified from scratch, and
        ; emit NOTHING. A decoder that cannot lose the clock is not decoding.
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   15, A
        JMP   main
bit_store:
        LDM   A, 7
        MOV   Y, A
        LDM   A, 12
        MOV   X, A
        MOV   A, Y
        ADD   A, X             ; the bit is placed with a general add of
        STM   12, A            ; two bytes, whose carry is NOT tested: both
        LDM   A, 7             ; are 0 or 1, so the sum never carries
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   7, A
        LDI   A, 8
        LDM   X, 7             ; THE SAME FAULT AS THE ONE EIGHT INSTRUCTIONS
                               ; ABOVE, IN THE SAME SHAPE: `LDI A, 8 / SUB A, 1`
                               ; computes 8 - 1 = 7, a CONSTANT, because SUB's
                               ; operand field is an IMMEDIATE and this ISA has
                               ; no `SUB A, <dmem>` form at all. So JZ byte_done
                               ; never fired, the bit counter climbed to 23 and
                               ; stopped, dmem[9] never advanced past 0, and
                               ; dmem[0..2] stayed 00 -- the firmware counted
                               ; bits and never finished a byte. The probe named
                               ; it: dmem[7] = 23 after 24 bits, dmem[9] never
                               ; moved. dmem[7] has to come through X to be on
                               ; the right of a subtraction.
        SUB   A, X             ; 8 - dmem[7]: zero at eight bits, a byte full
        JZ    byte_done
        JMP   main
byte_done:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter starts again for the next byte
        ; THE DISPATCH IS ON dmem[9] ALONE, and it was not. The first version
        ; computed `LDM A,12 / ADD A,X` and then tested the SUM for zero, then
        ; one, else two -- so it dispatched on `byte + index`, which is zero
        ; only when the byte happens to be zero. Measured: for the frame a5 3c
        ; 96 the stores went 0/1/2 = ZERO times to slot 0, once to slot 1 and
        ; once to slot 2, and dmem[0..2] came out 00 1c 1c. A byte that is not
        ; zero can never reach store0. The index was in X the whole time and
        ; nothing had clobbered it; the ADD was the only thing wrong, and it
        ; was the kind of line that reads as "compute the address" and is
        ; really "compute a value and then ask whether it is zero".
        LDI   A, 0
        LDM   X, 9
        SUB   A, X             ; zero iff dmem[9] is 0
        JZ    store0
        LDI   A, 1
        SUB   A, X             ; zero iff dmem[9] is 1
        JZ    store1
        JMP   store2
store0:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   0, A
        JMP   stored
store1:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   1, A
        JMP   stored
store2:
        LDM   A, 12
        STM   2, A
stored:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter starts again for the next byte
        LDM   X, 9
        LDI   A, 1
        ADD   A, X
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 3
        SUB   A, X             ; zero when THREE bytes have been banked
        JZ    frame_done
        JMP   main
        ; *** A FIFTH CONSTANT SUBTRACTION, AND THE THIRD OF THIS SHAPE ***
        ; This used to read `LDI A,3 / SUB A,1 / JZ enc_start`, which computes
        ; 3 - 1 = 2 -- a CONSTANT, because SUB's operand field is an IMMEDIATE
        ; and this ISA has no `SUB A, <dmem>` form at all. So the branch could
        ; never fire and enc_start was unreachable. In the source `SUB A, 1`
        ; reads like "subtract one"; in the listing a constant subtraction is
        ; visible as a constant. The other two of this shape are the bit
        ; counter's `LDI A,8 / SUB A,1` and the first_half test's
        ; `LDI A,2 / SUB A,1`, and all three were found by reading the listing
        ; rather than the source. The fix is the same in all three: the thing
        ; being compared has to come through X.
frame_done:
        ; THREE BYTES BANKED. The encoder is NOT entered from here: enc_start's
        ; half_wait sets dmem[13] to 0xFF and spins until it reads zero, which
        ; it never does, so handing over to it is a hang rather than an encode.
        ; Parking is the honest end state until the encoder is written, and the
        ; encode direction of the act stays red because it should.
        JMP   park

        ; ---- and then it encodes one byte on the other pad ---------------
enc_start:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   11, A
        LDI   A, 0x40
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; claim the output pad; the input is released
        LDM   A, 6
        STM   12, A            ; the byte to send
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   7, A             ; the bit counter
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   TXPIN, A         ; the line idles LOW while encoding
encoding:
        LDI   A, BMC_HALF
        STM   13, A
half_wait:
        LDM   A, 13
        JZ    half_end
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   13, A
        JMP   half_wait
half_end:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   13, A
        LDI   A, 1
        OUT   PINOE, A         ; drive the pad at the level A holds
        JMP   half_wait
park:
        JMP   park
